 2. GM shifts into Wall Street&#x27;s tech lane 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep ; Chart: Pete Gannon/Axios General Motors is moving its stock listing from the NYSE to Nasdaq, home to many of the world's biggest technology companies, Axios' Joann Muller writes .

 Why it matters: The switch reflects GM's growing emphasis on AI, autonomous driving and software-defined vehicles — with potential benefits for shareholders to boot. 🤖 What they're saying: "GM's customer-focused, technology-driven strategy is creating value for our stockholders, making Nasdaq a natural home for our listing as we grow," CFO Paul Jacobson said in a statement.

 Follow the money: The move also makes GM eligible for inclusion in the Nasdaq-100 index, which would attract new investment from funds that track the benchmark.

 💭 Joann's thought bubble: GM has been trying to recast its image as a technology company since 2016, when CEO Mary Barra and the Chevrolet Bolt EV appeared on the cover of Wired magazine for beating "Elon Musk in the race to build a true EV for the masses."

 Since then, GM's been arguing that it deserves a tech-company valuation — but Wall Street hasn't bought the pitch, continuing to treat it as a cyclical industrial company. The bottom line: GM will begin trading on Nasdaq on Nov. 17, retaining its familiar "GM" ticker.

 🥸 Changing exchanges won't change GM's underlying business. But it could help change how investors perceive it. 
 
